  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    ШЛЮЗ is a personal project, built outside client work. You are the operator at
    an airlock on a station 340 metres down. A visitor arrives, claims to be crew,
    and you decide: run the airlock cycle, refuse them, or flood the chamber.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interface is in Russian. It is playable in a browser with no install, and
    the whole thing loads in a couple of seconds because there are no assets to
    download beyond nine background frames.
  </p>

  <h2>The interesting constraint</h2>
  <p>
    Zero runtime dependencies, and no audio files at all. Every sound — the hull
    hum, radio static, sonar pings, knocking, the operator's own pulse and
    breathing — is generated by oscillators and filters at runtime through the Web
    Audio API. The CRT treatment is CSS and SVG noise rather than a shader.
  </p>
  <p>
    That started as a download-size decision and turned into the thing that made
    the audio reactive. The game tracks a fear level, and because the sound is
    synthesized rather than sampled, that level feeds straight into the scheduler:
    the operator's heart rate moves continuously from about 58 to 132 beats per
    minute. With audio files you would approximate that with a handful of loops and
    crossfades, and the seams would be audible exactly when the player is most
    attentive.
  </p>

  <h2>Architecture</h2>
  <p>
    The core is a rule engine that is entirely pure — no DOM, no audio, no
    module-level state. It takes a visitor, a rule and the station state, and
    returns a violation or nothing:
  </p>
  <pre><code>type Check = (visitor: Visitor, rule: Rule, station: StationState) =&gt; Violation | null;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Rules live in JSON as data — an id, the text shown to the player, a check type
    and parameters. Adding a rule is a data edit; adding a <em>kind</em> of rule is a
    new function. Most balance work never touches the logic.
  </p>
  <p>
    A violation carries the two facts that disagree rather than a boolean, so the
    interface renders the conflict directly and the message can never drift out of
    sync with what the check actually compared.
  </p>

  <h2>The design decision the game rests on</h2>
  <p>
    Every visitor has a <code>truth</code> field — what they actually are. The rule
    engine never reads it. It cannot tell you whether the thing at the airlock is a
    person; it only compares documents against records.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the engine knew, there would be a correct answer computable from the data and
    the game would collapse into an audit exercise. Withholding it means the rules
    are only ever evidence, and the judgement stays with the player — who has to
    weigh what the panels say against what they see through the porthole.
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    Everything: game design, engineering, audio synthesis, and the writing. Around
    thirty tests cover the rule engine, including tolerance boundaries and the case
    of a claim matching someone the roster lists as deceased.
  </p>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>
    Six shifts and four endings are playable end to end. There is a list of things
    that still feel wrong — pacing in the middle shifts, mostly — and the desktop
    build via Tauri is unstarted.
  </p>
  <p>
    I wrote in more detail about
    <a href="/writing/web-audio-game-sound-no-files">synthesizing the audio</a> and
    about <a href="/writing/pure-rule-engine-game-core">the rule engine</a>.
  </p>
